## §9 Update rules (read before editing this file)

### 📐 Compact item template

```
### <code> <status> [{{LABEL_HUMAN_ONLY}}|{{LABEL_ANYONE}}] <title — the item's identity; references point here>
**Remaining:** concrete work still to do (which repo/file/endpoint; if a human step, name who)
**Last evidence:** one line — date + sha / release / measurement
**Decision/blocker:** only what is STILL in force (owner decision, external approval, flag state)
**Trap:** ≤ 3 lines — what goes wrong if you do not know it (env name, order, silent failure)
**Full history:** `{{BOARD_ARCHIVE}} § <code>`
```

- **Replace evidence, do not append eras.** Old evidence goes to the archive under the same code; the live item shows only the current state.
- **Body ≤ 20 lines** (≤ 35 when it lists 5+ concrete sub-tasks).
- **Update the `###` heading in the same edit as the body.** Title and body go stale independently; a heading that still says 🔴 over a body that says "shipped" is the most common rot.
- **Never open a new `##`/`###` for a sub-note.** Sub-notes go into the item's fields or into the archive.
- **Look at the code before starting or closing an item.** The claim in the item is verified against the repo, not trusted.
- **Duplicate check before adding:** `git log -5`, grep the board for the slug, and check the coordination file — a concurrent session may already have written the same finding. If so mark yours `↪ DUPLICATE — see <code>` instead of adding a twin.

### 🔢 Item codes

New items use `<MMDD>-<short-slug>` (e.g. `0918-gate-baseline`), never a sequential number that two sessions could both pick. If a code collides, the first user keeps it; never renumber.

### 🚫 No derived views

No second hand-maintained status file. If a filtered view is needed, a script generates it and it is not committed.

### 📦 Archive discipline

A closed item's full text moves to `{{BOARD_ARCHIVE}}` under a `## <code>` heading in the same commit that marks it ✅. When the sentinel warns, archive closed items; never trim open ones.
